WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.538
Ken Watanabe: Alright, it’s 10:01, let’s get started. Happy Friday, everyone. Quick round of updates before we dive in?

2
00:00:09.436 --> 00:00:18.666
Nadia Petrova: Sure, I’ll go first. Sales Ops is wrapping up Q2 pipeline reviews, so it’s been a bit hectic. But nothing blocking from my side.

3
00:00:19.049 --> 00:00:26.741
Ethan Brooks: Deal Desk is mostly heads-down on a couple of big renewals. I’ll be out next Thursday, but otherwise, all good.

4
00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:36.410
Julia Costa: Billing’s been quiet, just the usual month-end stuff. Oh, and I finally got access to the new reporting dashboard, so thanks for that, Ken.

5
00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:44.904
Ken Watanabe: No problem, Julia. I’m glad that’s sorted. On my end, just prepping for the partner QBR next week. Nothing urgent.

6
00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:51.625
Nadia Petrova: Oh, Ken, did you see the Slack thread about the new partner onboarding checklist?

7
00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:56.539
Ken Watanabe: Yeah, I did. I’ll follow up after this call. Thanks for flagging.

8
00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.251
Ethan Brooks: Alright, should we jump into the main agenda item?

9
00:01:02.564 --> 00:01:10.256
Ken Watanabe: Let’s do it. So, we need to finalize the update to the Partner FAQ, specifically around auto-renewal for partner deals.

10
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:17.122
Nadia Petrova: Right, the current FAQ doesn’t actually mention auto-renewal at all, which has been confusing for the field.

11
00:01:18.169 --> 00:01:27.400
Julia Costa: Yeah, I’ve had a couple of partners ask if they’re on the hook for renewals if they miss the 30-day notice. It’s not clear.

12
00:01:28.303 --> 00:01:37.918
Ethan Brooks: Same here. I think we need to be explicit that auto-renewal isn’t enforced on partner deals. Otherwise, we get stuck in these back-and-forths every quarter.

13
00:01:38.137 --> 00:01:51.214
Ken Watanabe: Agreed. The draft I circulated says, basically, that when an order is placed through a partner, auto-renewal is deactivated. Partners and customers aren’t obligated to renew just because they didn’t send a cancellation notice.

14
00:01:51.536 --> 00:01:57.689
Nadia Petrova: And we’re adding that the partner should notify us if the end customer isn’t renewing, right?

15
00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:06.946
Ken Watanabe: Exactly. We want to make it clear that GitLab won’t take collection action if a partner deal lapses without formal cancellation.

16
00:02:07.453 --> 00:02:15.915
Julia Costa: Just to clarify, does this mean we’re not sending any dunning emails to partners if a renewal doesn’t come in on time?

17
00:02:16.643 --> 00:02:23.951
Ken Watanabe: Correct. For partner-transacted business, we don’t send dunning or collections. The partner manages the renewal process with their customer.

18
00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:34.392
Ethan Brooks: I have one concern—what if a partner forgets to notify us and the customer expects service to continue? How do we handle that edge case?

19
00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:48.793
Nadia Petrova: Good point. I think we should add a line that says, if the partner doesn’t notify us, we’ll reach out for confirmation before suspending service. That way, we’re not cutting anyone off by accident.

20
00:02:50.166 --> 00:02:57.474
Ken Watanabe: Yeah, I like that. We can add a sentence about confirming with the partner before any action is taken.

21
00:02:57.888 --> 00:03:02.119
Julia Costa: That works for me. It’ll help avoid awkward conversations with partners.

22
00:03:02.321 --> 00:03:06.552
Ethan Brooks: Agreed. As long as we’re not auto-renewing or auto-suspending, I’m good.

23
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:20.721
Ken Watanabe: Alright, so to recap: auto-renewal is not enforced on partner deals, partners and customers aren’t obligated to renew without notice, and we’ll confirm with the partner before suspending service if there’s no renewal or notification.

24
00:03:22.126 --> 00:03:29.818
Nadia Petrova: Got it. I’ll update the FAQ page with the new language and add that clarification about confirming with the partner.

25
00:03:31.039 --> 00:03:35.654
Ken Watanabe: Perfect. Nadia, can you get that done in the next two weeks?

26
00:03:36.225 --> 00:03:39.302
Nadia Petrova: Yep, I’ll have it updated by the 26th.

27
00:03:40.407 --> 00:03:44.254
Ken Watanabe: Awesome. Thanks, Nadia. Any other concerns before we move on?

28
00:03:45.403 --> 00:03:46.903
Julia Costa: Nope, I’m good.

29
00:03:48.122 --> 00:03:49.622
Ethan Brooks: All set here.

30
00:03:50.682 --> 00:03:55.682
Ken Watanabe: Alright, last thing—quick note on the team engagement survey. Results came in yesterday.

31
00:03:56.515 --> 00:03:59.592
Nadia Petrova: Oh, I haven’t seen those yet. Anything surprising?

32
00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:11.962
Ken Watanabe: Not really, but one thing that stood out was folks want more cross-team knowledge sharing. So, I’m proposing we do a short lunch-and-learn next month. I’ll send a poll for topics.

33
00:04:12.445 --> 00:04:18.214
Julia Costa: Love that idea. I’d be happy to share some billing tips if people are interested.

34
00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.336
Ethan Brooks: Yeah, I can do a session on deal structuring too.

35
00:04:23.493 --> 00:04:27.339
Ken Watanabe: Great, I’ll send out the poll by Monday. Thanks, everyone.

36
00:04:27.618 --> 00:04:30.310
Nadia Petrova: Thanks, Ken. Have a good weekend, all.

37
00:04:30.918 --> 00:04:32.418
Julia Costa: You too!

38
00:04:32.683 --> 00:04:34.183
Ethan Brooks: Bye, everyone.

39
00:04:35.003 --> 00:04:37.695
Ken Watanabe: Alright, closing out. See you next week.
